# Line endings: LF everywhere, in the repo and in the working tree.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in SYSTEM config, so every checkout on
# this machine got CRLF in the working tree while the repo stored LF. That is
# invisible until it is not: during the Step 7 dry run, `gitpush` Finish could not
# remove either worktree because `.claude/settings.local.json` showed as modified
# with no visible diff.
#
# A .gitattributes overrides core.autocrlf, so behaviour stops depending on a
# machine-wide setting that nothing in the repo can see — which matters more here
# than usual, because every worktree is a separate checkout and they all have to
# agree.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY LF AND NOT CRLF
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The repo is already all-LF: 724 of 724 text files are stored LF in the index.
# Everything that runs this code runs it on Linux — Vercel builds the React app,
# Supabase runs the Edge Functions under Deno — and the only Windows consumers are
# editors and PowerShell, both of which read LF without complaint. There are no
# .bat or .cmd files, which are the one thing that genuinely needs CRLF.
#
# So `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto` alone: auto normalises the repo but still
# writes CRLF into the working tree on Windows, which is the half that caused the
# trouble.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.mp3    binary
*.wav    binary
*.flac   binary
*.m4a    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.eot    binary

# Lockfiles are generated. Diffing them by hand helps nobody.
package-lock.json  -diff
